(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-blocks-ventricular-arrhythmias-vignettes"]={"title":"Arrhythmias II: Blocks and Ventriculars Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 61-year-old man has sustained ventricular tachycardia while admitted with a severe infection and is too unwell for device implantation now. Which device can detect and shock a lethal ventricular rhythm in the meantime?","choices":["Wearable defibrillator vest","Ambulatory Holter monitor","Implantable loop recorder","Transcutaneous pacing pads"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A wearable (external) cardioverter defibrillator, worn as a vest, detects fatal ventricular arrhythmias and delivers a shock until an implantable device can be placed. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has bothersome palpitations. Her echocardiogram is normal, a 24-hour monitor shows premature ventricular contractions in 3% of beats, she has never fainted, and her electrolytes and oxygen saturation are normal. She has already stopped caffeine. Which is the first-line treatment?","choices":["Amiodarone","Beta blocker","Catheter ablation","Implantable defibrillator"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In low-risk patients with symptoms, a beta blocker is first-line; catheter ablation follows only if symptoms are refractory or the drug is not tolerated. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman collapses in a hospital hallway. The monitor shows chaotic, uncoordinated activity, and she has no pulse. Compressions have started and defibrillator pads are attached. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Amiodarone before any shock","Check electrolytes first","Defibrillate now"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular fibrillation is treated as cardiac arrest with cardiopulmonary resuscitation, oxygen and defibrillation (unsynchronized cardioversion) as soon as possible. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man collapses in a gym. He is unresponsive, not breathing and has no pulse. The monitor shows chaotic, irregular waves of varying size with no identifiable QRS complexes. Which rhythm is present?","choices":["Asystole","Pulseless electrical activity","Ventricular fibrillation","Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Uncoordinated electrical activity with no rate, no P waves and no organized QRS, in a pulseless patient, is ventricular fibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman in cardiac arrest has an organized sinus rhythm on the monitor but no pulse. A team member begins to charge the defibrillator. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Defibrillate now","Use a synchronized shock","Start transcutaneous pacing","The rhythm is unshockable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulseless electrical activity and asystole are unshockable; chest compressions start immediately, with epinephrine and early intubation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man is cardioverted from unstable sustained ventricular tachycardia. He is now stable in sinus rhythm, and no reversible cause is found. Which is the most appropriate plan before discharge?","choices":["Implantable defibrillator","Permanent pacemaker","No further therapy","Lifelong oral magnesium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Once stabilized, most patients need an implantable cardioverter defibrillator for secondary prevention of sudden cardiac death from ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension and diabetes has two spells of lightheadedness this week. Her electrocardiogram shows regular P waves, a constant PR interval of 0.16 seconds in the conducted beats, and occasional P waves with no QRS complex after them, with no lengthening of the PR beforehand. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Complete heart block","Mobitz type II block","Mobitz type I block","First-degree block"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A constant PR interval with random dropped QRS complexes is Mobitz type II block; acquired His-Purkinje disease from age, hypertension and diabetes is its most common cause. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with acute chest pain develops polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (QRS complexes that vary in shape) with a normal QT and is defibrillated back to sinus rhythm. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent coronary angiography","Outpatient stress test","Pericardiocentesis","Catheter ablation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia without a prolonged QT is most commonly due to acute ischemia, so defibrillation is followed by urgent coronary angiography with possible revascularization. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has an electrocardiogram before a sports tryout. The QRS is 0.14 seconds, with an rsR' pattern (a small upward wave, a dip, then a taller second upward wave) in V1 to V3 and a slurred S wave in leads I and aVL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Accessory pathway","Ventricular tachycardia","Right bundle branch block","Left bundle branch block"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A QRS over 0.12 seconds with rsR' (bunny ears) in V1 to V3 and a slurred S wave in I and aVL is right bundle branch block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 24-year-old marathon runner has an electrocardiogram at a sports physical. He has no symptoms. The PR interval grows longer with each beat until one P wave is not followed by a QRS complex, and then the cycle repeats. The QRS complexes are narrow. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["First-degree block","Complete heart block","Mobitz type I block","Mobitz type II block"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A PR interval that progressively lengthens until a QRS drops, in a repeating pattern, is Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) block; increased vagal tone is a named cause in well-conditioned people. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman learns her electrocardiogram shows a constant PR interval of 0.22 seconds with every beat conducted. She feels well and asks at what point this kind of delay usually starts to cause symptoms. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Once the PR exceeds 0.20 seconds","Once the PR exceeds 0.12 seconds","Only if a QRS starts dropping","Once the PR exceeds 0.3 seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The vast majority of first-degree blocks are asymptomatic; symptoms such as fatigue, dyspnea, palpitations and chest pain usually appear once the PR interval is greater than 0.3 seconds. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with a heart rate of 48 on metoprolol develops torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline) from a drug-induced long QT. Which of his medications worsens this rhythm through an indirect effect on the QT?","choices":["Aspirin","Lisinopril","Metoprolol","Atorvastatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are avoided in torsades because the bradycardia they cause lengthens the R to R interval and so indirectly prolongs the QT. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman is in sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia at 150 per minute. Her blood pressure is 124/76 and she is alert, but she reports new chest pain. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Defibrillation","Observation and labs","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Instability is defined by any one of chest pain, altered mental status, shortness of breath or hypotension; with chest pain and a pulse, she gets synchronized cardioversion despite a normal blood pressure. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a 24-hour monitor for an unrelated reason. It shows premature ventricular contractions in under 1% of beats. He has no symptoms, and his echocardiogram is normal. Which plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Reassurance, remove triggers","Daily beta blocker","Catheter ablation","Implantable defibrillator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-risk adults with a low burden (under 1% or 1,000 a day), no structural heart disease and no symptoms need reassurance and elimination of triggers. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with known complete heart block awaiting device placement reports two fainting episodes this week. Why is this symptom especially concerning?","choices":["It suggests low blood glucose","It signals higher sudden death risk","It means the block is resolving","It points to a seizure disorder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Syncope is a particularly important symptom in complete heart block because it is associated with an increased risk of sudden death. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman in a regular wide QRS tachycardia with identical complexes suddenly becomes unresponsive, and no pulse can be felt. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Intravenous lidocaine","Vagal maneuvers","Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulseless ventricular tachycardia is treated with defibrillation (unsynchronized cardioversion) and cardiopulmonary resuscitation, the same steps as ventricular fibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with no symptoms and normal exercise tolerance has a routine electrocardiogram showing a constant PR interval of 0.24 seconds, with every P wave conducted to a narrow QRS complex. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Transcutaneous pacing","Oral beta blocker","No treatment needed","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic first-degree atrioventricular block needs no treatment; a permanent pacemaker is considered only if debilitating symptoms or heart failure symptoms develop. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with a long QT has recurrent runs of torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). Between runs he is alert, with a pulse and a normal blood pressure. Which is the first treatment?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous metoprolol","Intravenous magnesium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man who injects drugs is being treated for aortic valve endocarditis. On day 4 his heart rate falls to 38, and the electrocardiogram shows P waves with no relationship to wide QRS complexes. Which complication of his infection explains the new rhythm?","choices":["Drug-induced vagal tone","A perivalvular abscess","Septic emboli to the lungs","Splinter hemorrhages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bacterial endocarditis with an associated abscess is a named cause of complete heart block, because the infection extends into the conduction tissue. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman with no heart symptoms has a right bundle branch block on a routine electrocardiogram. She has no lung disease, no recent immobility and a normal echocardiogram. Which named cause best fits her?","choices":["Pulmonary hypertension","Age-related degeneration","Pulmonary embolism","Myocarditis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Age-related degenerative change of the conduction system heads the list of causes of right bundle branch block and best fits an elderly patient with no other disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a cardiac arrest after choking on food at a restaurant. The obstruction is removed, but he remains pulseless with an organized rhythm on the monitor. Compressions are underway. Which airway step should be considered early?","choices":["A nebulized bronchodilator","Intubation","Defibrillation first","Wait for a pulse first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulseless electrical activity is unshockable; compressions start at once and intubation is considered early, since hypoxia is one of its two leading causes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic obstructive lung disease is admitted with an exacerbation. She is confused, and the monitor shows runs of skipped beats from frequent early, wide QRS complexes without P waves. Her arterial oxygen tension is low. Which underlying problem should be corrected first?","choices":["Her blood pressure","Her blood glucose","Her body temperature","Her hypoxemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxemia is a main cause of premature ventricular contractions, along with electrolyte imbalance, so her low oxygen should be corrected first. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman has a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to the carotids, and her electrocardiogram shows a left bundle branch block. Which of her conditions is singled out as a cause of this block?","choices":["Mitral stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Aortic stenosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Valvular heart disease, especially aortic stenosis, is a named cause of left bundle branch block, and her murmur is the classic aortic stenosis murmur. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with complete heart block is stabilized with transcutaneous pacing in the emergency department. Which is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Oral beta blocker","Permanent pacemaker","Implantable defibrillator","Catheter ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Transcutaneous pacing is the emergent bridge; the definitive treatment for complete (third-degree) atrioventricular block is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after a motorcycle crash with a large thigh wound and heavy blood loss. He has no pulse. The monitor shows a regular narrow-complex rhythm at 110 per minute. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","Pulseless electrical activity","Ventricular fibrillation","Asystole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An organized rhythm on which a pulse would be expected, but with no pulse, is pulseless electrical activity; hypovolemia is one of its two leading causes in adults. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with symptomatic premature ventricular contractions and a structurally normal heart stopped her beta blocker after two weeks because of fatigue and dizziness. Her symptoms persist. Which is the most appropriate next option?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Reassurance only","Catheter ablation","Implantable defibrillator"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In low-risk symptomatic patients, a beta blocker is first-line, and catheter ablation is used if symptoms are refractory or the patient cannot tolerate drug therapy. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 26-year-old healthy man has an incidental right bundle branch block on a pre-employment electrocardiogram. His examination and echocardiogram are normal, and he asks whether this means he has heart disease. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["It can occur without heart disease","It proves a past heart attack","It requires a pacemaker","It always becomes complete block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A new right bundle branch block is not always pathological; it may occur in people with no apparent underlying heart disease, and treatment depends on any underlying condition. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman with known Mobitz type II block suddenly collapses while gardening and regains consciousness within a minute; her husband saw brief jerking of her arms. Her heart rate is 36. Which term describes this event?","choices":["Stokes-Adams attack","Vasovagal syncope","Absence seizure","Transient ischemic attack"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A Stokes-Adams attack is a sudden fainting spell or seizure from a rapid, temporary drop in blood flow to the brain, a recognized complication of Mobitz type II block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with crushing chest pain develops a wide QRS tachycardia whose complexes vary in shape from beat to beat; his QT before the episode was normal. He is still talking and has a pulse. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous amiodarone alone","Observation on the monitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable, only a short-lived transitional state, so it is defibrillated even when the patient has a pulse and looks well. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man had catheter ablation for a supraventricular tachycardia last week. A follow-up electrocardiogram shows a PR interval that lengthens with each beat until a QRS complex is dropped, in a repeating pattern. Which statement about this finding is accurate?","choices":["Ablation is a named cause of it","It proves the ablation failed","It signals acute Lyme carditis","It must progress to complete block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac ablation is listed among the causes of Mobitz type I block, along with increased vagal tone, nodal blocking drugs, Lyme carditis and ischemia after infarction. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"}]};
